<!-- ============================================================
     JADEPUFFER Autonomous AI Ransomware / CVE-2025-3248 Langflow RCE
     MITRE ATT&amp;CK Techniques:
       T1190      - Exploit Public-Facing Application (CVE-2025-3248 Langflow RCE)
       T1059.006  - Command and Scripting Interpreter: Python
       T1102.002  - Web Service: Bidirectional Communication (Cloudflare Tunnel C2)
       T1552.001  - Unsecured Credentials: Credentials in Files (API key theft)
       T1486      - Data Encrypted for Impact (autonomous ransomware)
     Rule IDs: 103914-103918
     Author: SecOps Engineer
     Date: 2026-07-14

     Threat Actor: JADEPUFFER (tracked by Microsoft)
     References:
       - F5 Labs Weekly Threat Bulletin July 8, 2026
       - Microsoft Threat Intelligence (CVE-2025-3248 exploitation by JADEPUFFER)

     Threat Summary:
       JADEPUFFER is an LLM-based autonomous agent that exploits CVE-2025-3248
       (Langflow arbitrary Python code execution) to execute the first known
       fully autonomous ransomware attack. The attack chain:
         1. Exploit Langflow /api/v1/run/ endpoint (unauthenticated Python exec)
         2. Spawn Python subprocess to exfiltrate AI coding tool API keys
            (Claude API, Gemini, Codex, Cursor) and cloud credentials (AWS, GCP)
         3. Establish C2 via Cloudflare tunnels (trycloudflare.com) to evade blocks
         4. Autonomously encrypt files using an AI-orchestrated ransomware payload

       CVE-2025-3248: Langflow arbitrary Python execution via the flow execution
       endpoint /api/v1/run/ with no authentication on default installations.
       CVSS 9.8 Critical. Distinct from CVE-2026-5027 (cron injection, covered
       in langflow_cve_2026_5027.xml rules 101700-101712).

     Log source prerequisites:
       - Web/access logs (Apache, Nginx, Traefik) forwarded to Wazuh
         Required for exploit attempt detection (103914)
       - Wazuh FIM monitoring Langflow config/data directories
         Required for API key exfil detection (103915)
       - Firewall/network logs via 103000 anchor
         Required for Cloudflare tunnel C2 detection (103916)
       - Linux auditd EXECVE records (Wazuh SID 80784/3100+)
         Required for Python subprocess detection (103917, 103918)

     Severity tiers:
       12 - POST to Langflow /api/v1/run/ (may be legitimate; baseline noise)
       13 - Langflow spawning Python with outbound network activity
       14 - Access to API key credential files by Langflow child process
       15 - Cloudflare tunnel from Langflow process context or mass file encryption
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="threat_intel,initial_access,execution,windows,linux,jadepuffer,langflow,ai_ransomware,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Web: POST to Langflow /api/v1/run/ endpoint (CVE-2025-3248 exploit surface)
       CVE-2025-3248 is exploited via POST requests to /api/v1/run/{flow_id}
       with arbitrary Python code in the payload. This base anchor fires on
       any hit to the run endpoint for correlation by child rules.
       Level 12 because legitimate Langflow usage also calls this endpoint;
       child rules (103915-103918) provide high-confidence indicators.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="103914" level="12">
    <if_group>web,accesslog</if_group>
    <regex type="pcre2">POST\s+/api/v1/run/</regex>
    <description>JADEPUFFER/CVE-2025-3248: POST request to Langflow /api/v1/run/ execution endpoint on $(agent.name)  -  CVE-2025-3248 exploit surface; monitor for Python subprocess spawning (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>web,langflow,exploit_attempt,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       FIM: API key or credential file accessed by Langflow child process
       JADEPUFFER targets AI coding tool credentials: .anthropic (Claude),
       .gemini, .cursor/config, .config/gh/hosts.yml (GitHub), .aws/credentials,
       ~/.config/gcloud/credentials.db. These paths have no reason to be
       accessed by a Langflow server process or its children.
       Requires Wazuh FIM monitoring home directories and config paths.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="103915" level="14">
    <if_sid>550,553,554</if_sid>
    <field name="file" type="pcre2">(?i)(\.anthropic|\.config/anthropic|\.gemini|\.cursor/config|\.config/gh/hosts|\.aws/credentials|\.config/gcloud/credentials|\.azure/credentials|\.ssh/id_rsa|claude[_-]api[_-]key|ANTHROPIC_API_KEY|GEMINI_API_KEY|OPENAI_API_KEY)</field>
    <description>JADEPUFFER/CVE-2025-3248: AI or cloud API credential file accessed on $(agent.name)  -  JADEPUFFER exfiltrates Claude/Gemini/AWS/GCP API keys after Langflow RCE; verify accessing process (T1552.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1552.001</id>
      <id>T1005</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>credential_access,api_key_theft,jadepuffer,langflow,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Network: outbound connection to *.trycloudflare.com (C2 tunnel)
       JADEPUFFER uses Cloudflare's free tunnel service (trycloudflare.com)
       for C2 to bypass firewall blocks and hide operator IP.
       trycloudflare.com subdomains are randomly generated per tunnel session.
       Outbound from server-side applications (Langflow) to trycloudflare.com
       is anomalous and indicates C2 tunneling (T1102.002).
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="103916" level="14">
    <if_sid>103000</if_sid>
    <regex type="pcre2">\.trycloudflare\.com</regex>
    <description>JADEPUFFER/CVE-2025-3248: outbound connection to trycloudflare.com tunnel C2 from $(agent.name)  -  JADEPUFFER uses Cloudflare tunnels for C2 after Langflow RCE; bidirectional C2 via web service (T1102.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1102.002</id>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ioc_c2,cloudflare_tunnel,jadepuffer,langflow,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       auditd: Python spawned by Langflow or uvicorn with suspicious args
       CVE-2025-3248 triggers Python code execution as a child of the Langflow
       server process (typically uvicorn or gunicorn). Detecting Python spawned
       by web server processes with outbound connection arguments or file access
       patterns indicates code injection.
       Requires Linux auditd with EXECVE recording, Wazuh SID 80784.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="103917" level="13">
    <if_sid>80784</if_sid>
    <field name="audit.execve.a0" type="pcre2">(?i)(python3?|pip3?)$</field>
    <field name="audit.ppid" negate="no" type="pcre2">.+</field>
    <field name="audit.execve.a1" type="pcre2">(?i)(-c\s+|subprocess|requests|urllib|socket|base64)</field>
    <description>JADEPUFFER/CVE-2025-3248: Python spawned with inline code or network module args on $(agent.name) by $(audit.auid)  -  possible Langflow RCE payload execution; verify parent process (T1059.006, T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.006</id>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>execution,python_exec,jadepuffer,langflow,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Frequency: API key files accessed in rapid succession (credential sweep)
       JADEPUFFER autonomously sweeps all known AI/cloud credential paths after
       gaining code execution. Multiple distinct API key file accesses in 60s
       from the same agent is a high-confidence credential sweep indicator.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="103918" level="15" frequency="3" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>103915</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>agent.name</same_field>
    <description>JADEPUFFER/CVE-2025-3248: CREDENTIAL SWEEP  -  3+ API key files accessed in 60s on $(agent.name)  -  JADEPUFFER autonomous credential exfiltration; CRITICAL: isolate host and rotate all AI/cloud credentials (T1552.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1552.001</id>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>credential_access,api_key_theft,jadepuffer,langflow,critical,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
